Zsh and Oh My Zsh

Install:
  Install missing Zsh and helper packages, Oh My Zsh and plugins; copy missing helper scripts and the theme.
  Apply the bundled .zshrc and register shell environment loading; set /bin/zsh as the login shell when the Zsh package is newly installed.

Upgrade:
  Update installed packages, Oh My Zsh and plugins; refresh helper scripts, the theme and .zshrc when Oh My Zsh or .zshrc is present.
  Refresh shell environment loading and preserve the login shell.

Status:
  Report package versions and Git revisions for Oh My Zsh and plugins; check whether .zshrc is present.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu, APT and a regular user with sudo access.

Configuration:
  Startup files: ${ZDOTDIR:-$HOME}/.zshrc and ${ZDOTDIR:-$HOME}/.zshenv
  ZSH: ~/.oh-my-zsh by default
  ZSH_CUSTOM: $ZSH/custom by default
  Path settings: ~/.config/wsley/zsh.sh; ZSH and ZSH_CUSTOM must be absolute paths and override saved values when supplied.
  A custom ZDOTDIR must be available in the shell startup environment.
  Install preserves existing helpers and themes; both install and upgrade replace a differing .zshrc with the bundled configuration.
  Helper commands support -h and --help. show-size displays disk usage as a tree; process helpers inspect processes and ports; Git helpers archive commits and manage WIP commits.
  The rm alias uses trash-cli; cleanup-dirs permanently removes tool caches, test environments, reports and empty directories, with an option to include .venv, node_modules and dist.
  docker-update-images pulls each local image tag once using the current Docker context; it requires Docker CLI and daemon access, and running containers keep their current images.
  Open a new Zsh session after installation or configuration updates.
